WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.105
Module two:

2
00:00:01.105 --> 00:00:02.547
wallets and security.

3
00:00:02.547 --> 00:00:06.529
Where coins really live, what a key and a seed phrase are,

4
00:00:06.529 --> 00:00:08.794
and how money is most often lost:

5
00:00:08.794 --> 00:00:12.158
not to a network hack, but to your own signature.

6
00:00:13.158 --> 00:00:14.950
A wallet does not hold coins:

7
00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:17.607
they are always recorded in the blockchain.

8
00:00:17.607 --> 00:00:19.399
A wallet holds a private key,

9
00:00:19.399 --> 00:00:22.859
a secret number that gives the right to sign a transfer.

10
00:00:22.859 --> 00:00:27.245
If the exchange holds the keys, you only have a record in its database.

11
00:00:27.245 --> 00:00:30.644
If only you hold the key, nobody can freeze your coins,

12
00:00:30.644 --> 00:00:33.486
but nobody can help you recover access either.

13
00:00:34.486 --> 00:00:36.554
Both options have their own risk.

14
00:00:36.554 --> 00:00:41.002
In November 2022 the FTX exchange halted withdrawals and went bankrupt:

15
00:00:41.002 --> 00:00:43.571
the company itself had used client money.

16
00:00:43.571 --> 00:00:48.896
And thousands of people have lost their seed phrase or sent coins to the wrong place,

17
00:00:48.896 --> 00:00:50.713
with no way to get them back.

18
00:00:50.713 --> 00:00:54.598
So the question is not whether it is safe, but whom you trust.

19
00:00:55.598 --> 00:00:57.728
A seed phrase is 12 or 24 words.

20
00:00:57.728 --> 00:01:02.587
From it the wallet derives all the keys, and from the keys the addresses.

21
00:01:02.587 --> 00:01:04.384
The arrows go only one way:

22
00:01:04.384 --> 00:01:06.913
you cannot find a key from an address.

23
00:01:06.913 --> 00:01:12.038
Whoever knows the phrase can restore the wallet anywhere and take everything.

24
00:01:13.038 --> 00:01:14.180
Hence three rules.

25
00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:16.019
The phrase cannot be changed:

26
00:01:16.019 --> 00:01:19.699
you can only create a new wallet and move the coins there.

27
00:01:19.699 --> 00:01:24.202
The phrase is never entered anywhere except to restore your own wallet:

28
00:01:24.202 --> 00:01:28.516
a request to enter it is a scam in a hundred cases out of a hundred.

29
00:01:28.516 --> 00:01:30.482
And the phrase is kept offline:

30
00:01:30.482 --> 00:01:33.654
on paper or metal, not in a photo or in the cloud.

31
00:01:34.654 --> 00:01:38.570
A hot wallet keeps the key on a device connected to the internet.

32
00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:40.860
A hardware wallet signs inside itself,

33
00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.692
and the key thing is to look at its own screen.

34
00:01:43.692 --> 00:01:45.741
Another trap is address poisoning:

35
00:01:45.741 --> 00:01:50.742
a scammer sends a tiny transfer from a similar address and waits for you to copy it

36
00:01:50.742 --> 00:01:51.766
from the history.

37
00:01:52.766 --> 00:01:55.050
The habits that save the most money.

38
00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:58.033
Before a large transfer, send a small test one.

39
00:01:58.033 --> 00:02:01.840
Take the address from a reliable place and check it in full.

40
00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:05.457
Keep a little in a hot wallet and the bulk in a cold one.

41
00:02:05.457 --> 00:02:06.536
And do not hurry:

42
00:02:06.536 --> 00:02:08.630
almost every scam pushes urgency.

43
00:02:09.630 --> 00:02:12.245
To swap a token on a decentralized exchange,

44
00:02:12.245 --> 00:02:15.394
you give its contract permission to spend that token.

45
00:02:15.394 --> 00:02:19.137
Often without a limit, and the permission stays after the swap.

46
00:02:19.137 --> 00:02:22.643
If the contract is hacked or the site turns out to be fake,

47
00:02:22.643 --> 00:02:25.317
the tokens are taken without a new signature.

48
00:02:25.317 --> 00:02:29.358
Set the limit to exactly the swap amount and revoke old permissions.

49
00:02:30.358 --> 00:02:35.139
Almost all losses of private individuals are not hacks but social engineering:

50
00:02:35.139 --> 00:02:39.492
the person hands over the phrase, a signature or a transfer themselves.

51
00:02:39.492 --> 00:02:41.085
Support that writes first.

52
00:02:41.085 --> 00:02:42.802
A lookalike site from an ad.

53
00:02:42.802 --> 00:02:46.970
A new acquaintance with their own platform you cannot withdraw from.

54
00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:49.606
Guaranteed income that is really a pyramid.

55
00:02:50.606 --> 00:02:51.629
The main points.

56
00:02:51.629 --> 00:02:55.271
Coins live in the blockchain, and a wallet holds the key:

57
00:02:55.271 --> 00:02:58.403
whoever knows the key or the phrase is the owner.

58
00:02:58.403 --> 00:03:02.749
On an exchange you trust the exchange; in your own wallet, yourself.

59
00:03:02.749 --> 00:03:07.286
And money is most often lost to a signature the person gave themselves.
